# Module structure

## Module template

New modules, and modules being refactored, use this one shape:

```txt
<module>/
  index.js        module entry: register on _KDB, controller, mount/destroy
  state.js        action types + initial state + reducer (pure)
  selectors.js    read functions; the only file other modules import
  view.js         DOM build, place and sync (or view/ by screen area when large)
  effects.js      Cognos calls, event emits, history commits, async work
  config.js       constants, defaults, label keys, selector strings, error codes
  history.js      optional: undo/redo steps (kit pushHistoryStep) and replay handler (kit createReplayHandler)
  restore.js      optional: parameter-set restore handler
  tabMutation.js  optional: tab move/duplicate/delete participation (uses core/utils/filters/tabMutationRun)
  events.js       optional: names of events this module emits to others
```

- Create only files with real content. Small modules (toolbar controls, pageSurface, authoring, stubs) can be just `index.js` plus one or two files.
- Large modules split by **sub-domain**, not by step: for example `widgetVis/binding.js`, `widgetVis/drillthrough.js`, `widgetVis/topN.js`, or a folder `binding/` once it holds about 4 or more files. Same for a large `restore/` or `view/`.
- At most 3 folder levels below the module folder.
- Services use the same idea: `index.js` builds the named-method service object; pipeline services (writeback `get/` and `set/`, widgetBindingPlan) keep their sub-domain folders.
- `selectors.js` and `events.js` are the only cross-module import targets. Everything else is private to the module.
  - `selectors.js` may also publish the owner's constants (`ACTION_TYPES`, DOM selector registry). Transitional exception: `widgetSettings/selectors.js` also publishes a settings-panel kit (`buildSettingsIcon`, `markSelectedOption`, `shouldKeepDropdownsOpen`, `resolveWidgetEl`, `dispatchDropdownOptionIntent`, `dispatchIconIntent`, `startWidgetRenderLoading`) for the widgetVis focus panel; it goes away when the intent and loading calls move behind `api.widgetSettings.dispatch` and the focus panel is hosted in widgetSettings (see the header of that file). Do not add to it.
- `core/utils/` holds pure helpers only. Code with run ids, snapshots or cancellation is not a util (`core/utils/filters/tabMutationRun/` is saga machinery and is not published on `_KDB.utils`).
- No magic entry names in new or refactored code: no `__controller`, no new `__orchestrator*`. Name functions after what they do (`createHeaderController`, `registerHeader`, `applyHeaderRestore`).

### `index.js` skeleton

```js
define([
  "./state.js", "./view.js", "./effects.js", "../tabs/events/eventNames.js",
  "../../core/utils/kit/runtime.js", "../../core/utils/kit/errors.js"
], function (_state, _view, _effects, _tabsEvents, _rt, _errors) {
  "use strict";
  // Lifecycle terms: idle, mounted, disposed.

  function createHeaderController(_KDB) {
    var guard = _errors.createDisposedGuard("header");   // warns once per method after destroy
    var rt = _rt.ensureRuntime(_KDB, "header");          // get-or-create: keeps the facet stamps

    function mount(options) {
      if (guard.blocked("mount")) return;
      var scope = new AbortController();
      rt.abortControllers.mount = scope;
      _view.renderHeader(_KDB, getState(), options || {});
      _rt.addListener(rt.listeners, "windowResize", window, "resize", onWindowResize);
      _KDB.events.subscribe(_tabsEvents.CHANGED, onTabsChanged, { signal: scope.signal });
    }

    function dispatch(action) {
      if (guard.blocked("dispatch")) return { ok: false, errorCode: "HEADER_DISPOSED" };
      try {                                              // public entry point = boundary
        _KDB.state.header = _state.reduceHeader(_KDB.state.header, action);
        return _effects.runHeaderEffects(_KDB, action);
      } catch (err) {
        console.error("[header.dispatch] " + action.type + " failed", err);
        return { ok: false, errorCode: "HEADER_DISPATCH_FAILED" };
      }
    }

    function getState() { return guard.isDisposed() ? undefined : _KDB.state.header; }

    function destroy() {
      if (guard.isDisposed()) return;
      guard.dispose();                                   // first, so in-flight callbacks stop
      var errors = _rt.disposeRuntimeBuckets(rt);        // aborts, unbinds, clears timers; keeps stamps
      if (errors.length) console.error("[header.destroy] cleanup errors", errors);
      _view.removeHeader(_KDB);
    }

    function onWindowResize() { dispatch({ type: "header/syncLayout" }); }
    function onTabsChanged(payload) { dispatch({ type: "header/syncActiveTab", payload: payload }); }

    return { mount: mount, destroy: destroy, dispatch: dispatch, getState: getState };
  }

  function registerHeader(_KDB) {
    _KDB.state.header = _KDB.state.header || _state.createInitialHeaderState();
    _rt.ensureRuntime(_KDB, "header").dashboardFacet = _KDB.services.cognosHost.dashboard;
    _KDB.api.header = createHeaderController(_KDB);
  }

  return registerHeader;
});
```

Never replace `_KDB.runtime.<owner>` wholesale; stamps and owner fields live next to the buckets. Timer id maps nested in `timers` are named `<thing>By<Key>` (for example `watchdogsByMountKey`) so `disposeRuntimeBuckets` cancels them; use `scheduleTimeout` / `scheduleFrame` for single timers. Until Phase 2 adds central replay registration, `history.js` still publishes its handler as `rt.historyReplayHandlerContribution`.

### Mapping from the current layout

| Today | Template |
|---|---|
| `__orchestrator<M>.js` + `create<M>Controller.js` + `runtime/ensure*`, `cleanup*` + `lifecycle/` | `index.js` (kit `ensureRuntime`, `disposeRuntimeBuckets`, `createDisposedGuard`) |
| nested sub-orchestrators (`kdbControl/{framework,settings,authoringMode,effects}/__orchestrator*.js`, `header` / `footer` `effects/__orchestrator*Effects.js`) | `index.js` or the sub-domain file they set up |
| `config/actionTypes.js`, `ACTION_*` constants in `events/eventNames.js`, `state/initialState.js`, `state/create*Reducer.js`, reducer helpers | `state.js` |
| `state/selectors.js` | `selectors.js` |
| other `config/*`, `compat/` config readers | `config.js` |
| `history/*` (create handler, apply, build, push, read snapshot) | `history.js` with kit `pushHistoryStep` and `createReplayHandler` |
| `actions/handleParameterSetRestore.js` + `actions/parameterSet/*` | `restore.js` (or `restore/` if large) |
| `actions/tabMutation/*` | `tabMutation.js` using `core/utils/filters/tabMutationRun` |
| `observation/` | `observation.js` (or a sub-domain file) |
| `adapters/` | `effects.js` or a sub-domain file; keep the folder only if it holds about 4+ files (writeback `map/adapters/`) |
| `view/build*`, `sync*`, `place*` | `view.js` (or `view/` by screen area) |
| `effects/*`, `actions/*` | `effects.js` or sub-domain files |
| `effects/read*Facet.js` | delete; read the stamp `_KDB.runtime.<owner>.<x>Facet` where it is used |

## Phase 1 transition: path-preserving shims

Phase 1 workstreams change only their own folders. Two kinds of files are imported from outside a module, and their paths must not move yet:

- **Entry files.** `KDB.js` imports every feature `__orchestrator<M>.js`; `core/services/__orchestratorServices.js` imports every service `__orchestrator<S>.js`. Neither `KDB.js` nor `__orchestratorServices.js` is edited in Phase 1.
- **Deep imports.** Any other file of your module that another module imports (published selectors, event names, and about 30 deep imports of `config/`, `view/`, `effects/`, `actions/` files).

When you move such a file's code, leave a one-line shim at the old path that only re-exports the new location, marked for removal:

```js
// shim: path kept for imports from outside this module; remove in Phase 2
define(["../view.js"], function (_view) { return _view.buildSettingsIcon; });
```

These shims are the **only** allowed forwarding files. Phase 2 rewrites the importers and deletes the shims. To find the imports into your module, run from the repo root `node tools/check-imports.mjs --json` (the `crossModule` and `coreToDashboard` lists) and list the files with a search from outside the folder, for example `rg -n "widgetSettings/" _____________KDB3API/KDB3/js --glob '!**/widgetSettings/**'`.

Modules not yet refactored keep their current layout (`config/ state/ view/ effects/ actions/ history/ runtime/ observation/ adapters/`). Do not add new files in that style to a module you are converting.

## File size guideline

Split by cohesion, not by count. A file holds one concept: a reducer and its action types, a view, one saga, one service facade, one config table.

| Measure (code lines; blank lines and comments do not count) | Meaning |
|---|---|
| up to about 300 to 400 | normal |
| over 500 | check whether the file holds two concepts |
| over 800 | justify in a header comment why it stays one file |
| function over about 60 lines | split into named steps |

- Lines over 180 characters count as packing; un-pack them. Never compress code to meet a size target.
- Do not create a file that only forwards to another file, re-exports it, or wraps it in try/catch (except the Phase 1 shims above).
- Merge tiny files (under about 30 lines with one importer) into their caller or their concept's file.
- Do not create a folder for fewer than about 4 files unless it is a named sub-domain.
- No vague names for files or folders: `helpers`, `common`, `misc`, `manager`, `shared`, `stuff`, nor overflow suffixes (`Support`, `Helpers`, `Internals`).
- Measure with `node tools/line-report.mjs --module <path>` (repo root).

`KDB.js` holds bootstrap wiring only: AMD imports, core setup, module registration, mount and destroy order, root namespaces. Changes to render/destroy semantics, the `_KDB` root shape or the renderer API need a written note and review (see `core.mdc` section 9).
